      "conclusion": "The library compresses correctly and verifiably (MDL falls, expansion is exact), and the compressed program is reachable one node sooner -- but in this language the search does not get cheaper, because adding an arity-1 constructor multiplies the number of programs at every size while the step it names is worth a single node of bound. Measured across bounds 4-10 the extended enumeration is 2-4x larger than the base one at the reduced bound. Applying the bound reduction by default would have made search ~2.3x more expensive while looking like a successful demonstration, so it is off by default. The trade depends on how large a share of a program the repeated structure is: see the dense-corpus rows."
